Z||000002||fierospeeder||01-01-2000||12:06 AM||||check if your egr valve is sticking, check if your spark plugs are all firing if you have a timing gun to see if it fires for each one,  check if all vacuum lines are connected.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-01-2000||01:11 AM||||A vacuum/EGR leak usually causes lean mix. not rich.<P>The 84 uses the 300 TBI like all the rest of the Fiero 4's thru 86.<P>A leaking injector, a sticking fuel presure regulator, or plugged return line could cause rich mix. A lagging IAC, or worn TPS, could also cause it.<P>you need to watch for exactly when the code sets. What are you doing? stopped at a long light? running highway speed? This is the kind of problem an ECM scanner comes in handy for. Knowing when the code sets tells you allot.<P><BR>For example. My 87SC 4 sets a 45 when I'm idling at a long light. However it only does this on very cold days under certain conditions. Condition 1. The car has only runX number of minutes. Condition 2. the car has run to temp but has been driven at highway speed for some time then stopped at a long light.<P>End result...... GM deleted the 4 cylinders Thermac in 87. My problem is caused by what is commonly known as "Carburator Icing." The IAC passage ices up making the mix go rich. as soon as I drive away from the light the code clears untill the throttle body gets cold and stops at another long light.<P>Cure... I need to put a Thermac set back on the 87. Once I can heat up the intake air in the winter the problem will stop.||||reg||1
